osystem\nL59: \nL60: cite20†Datasets Access high-quality datasets to accelerate your research. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L61: \nL62: cite30†Conferences & events L63: \nL64: Careers\nL65: ## Shaping the future together\nL66: \nL67: cite23†See all programs L68: \nL69: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L70: \nL71: cite31†Blog L72: \nL73: About\nL74: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL75: \nL76: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L77: \nL78: cite32†Google Research L79: \nL80: cite33†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite34†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI†deepmind.google cite35†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google L81: \nL82: [Button: Research ]\nL83: \nL84: [Button: Resources ]\nL85: \nL86: cite30†Conferences & events L87: [Button: Careers ]\nL88: \nL89: cite31†Blog L90: \nL91: [Button: About ]\nL92: \nL93: [Input: Search]\nL94: \nL95: Search\nL96: # Characterizing VLA Models: Identifying the Action Generation Bottleneck for Edge AI Architectures\nL97: \nL98: Manoj Vishwanathan\nL99: \nL100: Anand Raghunathan\nL101: \nL102: Suvinay Subramanian\nL103: \nL104: 2026\nL105: \nL106: cite36†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L107: ## Abstract\nL108: This study characterizes the performance of Vision-Language-Action (VLA) workloads on next-generation edge hardware to address the critical \"Memory Wall\" in embodied AI. Profiling of the Molmo-7B model confirms that auto-regressive Action Generation is strictly memory-bound, with standard compute scaling yielding diminishing returns (4x compute scaling but only 1.25x latency reduction).\nL109: We evaluate system scalability across LPDDR6, GDDR7, and HBM3e interfaces, finding that standard memory technologies fail to meet the 10Hz real-time safety target for models larger than 13B. Specifically, a 100B parameter model on a GDDR7-equipped system incurs an unsafe latency of 83 seconds.\nL110: In contrast, our analysis shows that Processing-in-Memory (PIM) architectures, by leveraging internal bank-level parallelism, deliver a transformative 140x speedup, reducing 100B model latency to 2.8 seconds and bringing large-scale reasoning within the realm of feasibility for robotic control.\nL111: ×\n"
